    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Esto muestra una característica importante de los hiperparámetros:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important}\n",
        "## Los hiperparámetros interactúan\n",
        "\n",
        "No tiene mucho sentido buscar el ``mejor `learning_rate`'' de manera completamente independiente del número de árboles.\n",
        "\n",
        "Los hiperparámetros pueden **interactuar entre sí**.\n",
        "\n",
        "Esta observación será fundamental cuando estudiemos Grid Search y Random Search.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Complejidad de los árboles\n",
        "\n",
        "También podemos modificar la complejidad de cada árbol.\n",
        "\n",
        "En este caso utilizaremos `max_depth`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Comparemos:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\texttt{max\\_depth}\\in\\{1,2,3,5\\}.\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "c3671578"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "profundidades = [1, 2, 3, 5]\n",
        "\n",
        "resultados_depth = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for depth in profundidades:\n",
        "    \n",
        "    modelo = GradientBoostingRegressor(\n",
        "        n_estimators=100,\n",
        "        learning_rate=0.1,\n",
        "        max_depth=depth,\n",
        "        random_state=42\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    \n",
        "    modelo.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    pred = modelo.predict(X_train)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    rmse = np.sqrt(\n",
        "        mean_squared_error(y_train, pred)\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    \n",
        "    resultados_depth.append({\n",
        "        \"max_depth\": depth,\n",
        "        \"RMSE_train\": rmse\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.DataFrame(resultados_depth)"
      ],
